2026-05-28T12:04Z HDMX-coding-agent created

--- body ---
## Context

The capability-timeline material uses three different stage numberings
that disagree on which integer points to which capability. PR #632
(label rename) flagged this drift but did not fix it.

The three numberings observed today:

1. **Data + figure** — `data/capability_timeline.csv` and
   `src/aedist/plot_capability_timeline.py::FEATURE_LABELS`:
   1 Chat LLM, 2 Web search, 3 Code execution, 4 Files upload (RAG),
   5 Reasoning, 6 External tool use (MCP), 7 Deep research,
   8 Autonomous agents.
2. **Prose schema + matrix** — `docs/capability-timeline.md`:
   1 Chat LLM, 2 Files upload (RAG), 3 Web search, 4 Reasoning,
   5 Deep research, 6 Code execution, 7 External tool use (MCP),
   8 Autonomous agents.
3. **Proposal draft** — `docs/section3-proposal.md` (ticket 0219):
   uses yet another scheme labelling file-upload rows as
   "2 Retrieval / file upload" (now stale string and stale number).

The data/figure numbering is the source of truth — it is what readers
of the paper see on the figure y-axis, and it is the structure tests
key off. The doc and proposal need to be brought into line with it
(or, if the prose numbering is preferred, the CSV/py need to change;
that path is harder because the data file is a tracked artefact and
re-numbering touches every row).

## Relevant files

- `data/capability_timeline.csv` — source of truth (stage column)
- `src/aedist/plot_capability_timeline.py` — `FEATURE_LABELS` dict
- `docs/capability-timeline.md` — schema list (lines ~70–85) and
  matrix (lines ~97–106)
- `docs/section3-proposal.md` — lines 175, 182 (and possibly more)
- `tests/test_capability_timeline_sync.py` — currently checks dates
  only; should be extended to enforce stage-number agreement

## Actions

1. Decide which numbering is authoritative — recommend keeping the
   CSV/py order since the figure is the user-facing artefact and the
   CSV is the tested data file.
2. Re-number the prose schema and matrix in
   `docs/capability-timeline.md` to match the CSV order (8 row swaps
   in the matrix, 8 list-item renumbers in the schema, plus any prose
   that cross-references "stage N" within the doc).
3. Audit `docs/section3-proposal.md` — either renumber the table rows
   in place or mark the doc as historical and add a one-line note at
   the top pointing to the current schema.
4. Extend `tests/test_capability_timeline_sync.py` with an assertion
   that every `**N. <label>**` heading in the prose matrix matches the
   CSV's `(stage, stage_name)` pair, so future renames cannot
   re-introduce the same drift silently.

## Test

Extend `tests/test_capability_timeline_sync.py` with a new
`@pytest.mark.adherence` test that parses the prose matrix headers
(regex `\*\*(\d+)\. ([^*]+?)\*\*`) and asserts each `(stage_number,
label_string)` tuple appears as a `(stage, stage_name)` row in
`data/capability_timeline.csv`. The test should fail today on the
unaligned doc, then pass after the renumbering.

## Verification

- [ ] `pytest tests/test_capability_timeline_sync.py` passes including
      the new assertion
- [ ] Visual diff of the rendered prose matrix shows stage numbers
      now match the figure y-axis numbering
- [ ] `docs/section3-proposal.md` either reflects the canonical
      numbering or carries a "historical, see capability-timeline.md"
      banner

## Invariants

- `data/capability_timeline.csv` row content (dates, sources, notes)
  must not change — only the prose and the test change
- The rebuilt `report/inputs/generated/fig_capability_timeline.pdf`
  must remain byte-identical to the version landed in PR #632 (the
  figure depends only on the CSV, which is unchanged)
- Existing test `test_csv_dates_present_in_prose` must continue to
  pass

## Exit criteria

- All three documents use the same stage numbering
- Sync test enforces the alignment so future drift fails fast
- PR merged and `tests/test_capability_timeline_sync.py` green on main
